changes.rationale:
**The Hishtaphel of *chwh* (formerly mis-parsed as the Hithpael of *shachah*; HALOT and the modern Hebrew lexica register the corrected stem-analysis after the discovery of the Ugaritic *yštḥwy* parallel) is the standard HB worship/prostration/obeisance verb — hundreds of occurrences across the corpus, governing every standard prostration / worship / homage-to-superior scene. The Genesis-WoH translation deploys the verb at its Yosef-cycle dream-and-fulfilment-arc node: the sheaves dream at Gen 37:7 (*va-tishtachaveynah la-alumati*, 'and they made obeisance to my sheaf'), the sun-moon-and-stars dream at Gen 37:9 (*mishtachavim li*, 'making obeisance to me'), Ya'aqov's incredulous Gen 37:10 (*le-hishtachavot lekha artzah*, 'to bow down to thee to the earth'), and the first fulfilment at Gen 42:6 (*va-yishtachavu-lo apayim artzah*, 'and made obeisance to him with their faces to the earth') with the narrator's explicit dream-fulfilment-flag at v 9 (*va-yizkor Yosef et ha-chalomot asher chalam lahem*, 'and Yosef remembered the dreams which he dreamed of them'). The forward fulfilment-verses continue: Gen 43:26, 28; 44:14; 50:18.** // **Promotion-to-central history.** This entry was originally housed in the Genesis-WoH overlay as `hishtachavah-obeisance-yosef-dreams`, scoped to the Gen 37 dream-verses, with the explicit annotation: *strong promotion candidate; when the Yosef-cycle fulfilment-verses (Gen 42:6; 43:26 + 28; 44:14; 50:18) and the wider worship-corpus are in view, a central `hishtachavah-bow-down-obeisance-worship` entry should be created, with this Gen 37 entry as the dream-anchor sibling*. The promotion was scheduled to happen at the long-anticipated Gen 42:6 fulfilment-moment, and the v 2.42.0 Genesis 42 editorial pass executes that promotion: the overlay entry has been deprecated and removed; this central entry takes over the Gen 37 anchor-verses AND extends to Gen 42:6 (the first fulfilment) AND will extend forward to the Gen 43-50 fulfilment-verses when those chapters are translated. // **The lexeme.** The verb is the Hishtaphel (an old reflexive-causative stem, attested across Northwest Semitic; cf. Ugaritic *yštḥwy*) of the root *chwh* ('to bow down'). The stem-analysis matters because the former Hithpael-of-*shachah* parse generated the wrong morphological-history: the verb is not a Hithpael of a separate root but the Hishtaphel of a single Northwest Semitic *chwh*-root. HALOT s.v. *chwh* (Hishtaphel) records the corrected analysis; J. A. Emerton 'The Etymology of *hištaḥawāh*' OTS 20 (1977) 41-55 is the standard treatment. The verb means 'to bow oneself down, to prostrate oneself, to do homage/obeisance' — the full-body prostration of respect, submission, or worship. The form is reflexive-causative: the action of causing-oneself-to-bow-down. // **The semantic range.** The lexeme covers three distinguishable but overlapping registers: (i) **prostration before a human superior** — before a king, a powerful person, or one whose authority is being acknowledged (Gen 23:7, 12 — Avraham before the Hittites; Gen 27:29 — the brothers-shall-bow-down formula in Yitzchaq's blessing of Ya'aqov; Gen 33:3, 6, 7 — Ya'aqov, the wives, the children before Esav; Gen 37:7, 9, 10 — the Yosef-cycle dreams; Gen 42:6 the first fulfilment; many others); (ii) **prostration before the deity in worship** — the standard worship-verb (Gen 22:5 — Avraham at the Aqedah, *ve-nishtachaveh*, 'we will worship'; Gen 24:26, 48, 52 — Avraham's servant; Exod 4:31 — Mosheh and Aharon; the Pentateuchal cultic-instructional uses; the Psalmic worship-verses; etc.); (iii) **prostration before idols / other deities** — the standard prohibited-worship verb of the Decalogue and the prophetic indictments (Exod 20:5 — *lo tishtachaveh lahem*; Deut 5:9; the prophets' indictments of idolatry). The choice of register is context-determined; the lexeme itself does not discriminate. // **The dream-and-fulfilment-arc deployment in the Yosef cycle.** The verb is the structural keyword of the Yosef cycle: the brothers' obeisance-to-Yosef in the dreams of Gen 37 is fulfilled in the obeisance-to-Yosef as the Egyptian *shallit* at Gen 42:6, 43:26, 28; 44:14; 50:18. Each fulfilment-occurrence uses the same verb the dreams used. The narrator's explicit flag at Gen 42:9 (*va-yizkor Yosef et ha-chalomot*) makes the connection between dream and fulfilment textually unambiguous. The cycle's literary structure is built on the lexeme's recurrence: the brothers' refusal to acknowledge Yosef's lordship at Gen 37:8 (*ha-malokh timlokh aleinu im-mashol timshol banu* — 'shalt thou indeed reign over us, or shalt thou indeed have dominion over us?') is reversed by their unwitting submission at Gen 42:6 (the *apayim artzah* full-prostration). The cycle resolves the dispute about the dreams not by argument but by performance: the brothers bow. // **The *apayim artzah* full-prostration formula.** The Gen 42:6 verse adds the *apayim artzah* ('with their faces to the earth') intensifier — the standard full-prostration formula, distinct from a lesser bow. The formula recurs at Gen 19:1; 33:3; Num 22:31; 1 Sam 20:41; 24:8 [MT 24:9]; 25:23; 28:14; 2 Sam 9:6; 14:4, 22, 33; 18:28; 24:20; 1 Kgs 1:23, 31; 1 Chr 21:21; 2 Chr 7:3; 20:18 — always marking the most formal prostration, often before a king or the deity. // **Central housing.** **MANDATORY central** by (i) the lexeme's status as the corpus's standard worship/prostration verb (hundreds of occurrences, governing every standard prostration / worship / homage-to-superior scene); (ii) the load-bearing function in the Yosef cycle's structural dream-and-fulfilment arc (Gen 37:7, 9, 10 / 42:6 / 43:26, 28; 44:14; 50:18 — one of the corpus's tightest cross-chapter lexical-keyword arcs); (iii) the necessary single governing decision for the verb across all its registers (human-superior / deity-worship / idolatrous-worship) and across all its occurrences. *AppliesTo* is anchored at the Gen 37 dream-verses and the Gen 42:6 first fulfilment, with forward extension to Gen 43-50 fulfilment-verses and the wider corpus-worship/prostration scenes to be added when those texts are translated. // **The translation choice.** The WoH translation renders the ASV-baseline **'made obeisance to'** consistently across the dream-and-fulfilment-arc: 'made obeisance to my sheaf' (Gen 37:7); 'making obeisance to me' (Gen 37:9); 'to bow down to thee to the earth' (Gen 37:10); 'made obeisance to him with their faces to the earth' (Gen 42:6). The consistency-decision preserves the dream-fulfilment lexical-keyword across the cycle. The plainer 'bowed down' (NJPS, Alter) and the literal 'prostrated themselves' are noted as alternatives; the 'worshipped' rendering is reserved for the unambiguously cultic-deity registers elsewhere in the corpus (not the Gen 37-50 obeisance-to-Yosef scenes, which are homage-to-human-superior, not worship). // The claim_type is *direct* because (a) the verb is morphologically explicit (the Hishtaphel of *chwh*); (b) the lexeme's status as the corpus's standard prostration-and-worship verb is documented across HALOT, BDB, TDOT, NIDOTTE; (c) the Gen 37 dreams and the Gen 42:6 fulfilment use the same lexeme — the dream-and-fulfilment lexical structure is grammatically transparent; (d) the cross-corpus distribution is verifiable; (e) the 'made obeisance' rendering is the direct sense in the homage-to-superior register. The 'worship' overlap with the cultic register is documented but not adopted on the surface for the Yosef-cycle scenes (which are homage, not worship). Named scholarship only; no project synthesis. // **Scholarly literature.** HALOT s.v. *chwh* (Hishtaphel); BDB p. 1005 (s.v. *shachah*, with the now-superseded root-analysis); TDOT IV:248-256; NIDOTTE 2:42-44; J. A. Emerton 'The Etymology of *hištaḥawāh*' OTS 20 (1977) 41-55 (the Ugaritic *yštḥwy* analysis); Sarna *JPS Genesis* 1989 pp. 256, 290; Westermann *Genesis 37-50* 1986 pp. 37, 106-107; Alter *The Five Books of Moses* 2004 pp. 208, 242; on the verb as the structural keyword of the Yosef cycle: G. W. Coats *From Canaan to Egypt: Structural and Theological Context for the Joseph Story* CBQMS 4, 1976; on the Gen 37:8 / 42:6 reversal-structure: J. P. Fokkelman *Narrative Art in Genesis* SSN 17, 1975; on the prostration formula *apayim artzah*: M. I. Gruber *Aspects of Nonverbal Communication in the Ancient Near East* Studia Pohl 12, 1980.

// **Gen 50:18 cycle-closing implicit-bowing extension.** *AppliesTo* extended to GEN-WOH-50:18 even though the surface lexeme there is *va-yippelu lefanav* ('they fell down before him') rather than the *hishtachavah*-form proper. The fall-down construction at v 18 is the cycle's narrative-closure of the Gen 37 sheaves-and-stars dream-fulfilment arc — the brothers' final prostration before Yosef after Ya'aqov's death. Named scholarship: Sarna *JPS Genesis* p. 351; Alter *Five Books of Moses* p. 299; Hamilton *NICOT Genesis 18-50* p. 699; Brueggemann *Genesis* IBC p. 376 (all reading v 18 as the narrative-completion of the dream-fulfilment-bowing-sequence even with the lexical shift). Including v 18 in the *appliesTo* preserves the lexical-keyword arc as a literary-observational matter; the rationale documents the lexical-shift explicitly.

// **Former Prophets clause (v2.84.0, wave-FP1 Editor pass).** The 'made obeisance' rendering was fixed for the Yosef-cycle dream-and-fulfilment arc, where it preserves the keyword across Gen 37-50. The Kings volumes, ratified at human sign-off on 2026-11-01, print plain 'bowed down' where the verb stands alone (1 Kgs 22:54; 2 Kgs 2:15; 17:35, 36) and idiomatic variants where it is paired or reflexive ('bowed himself', 1 Kgs 1:23, 47; 2:19; 2 Kgs 4:37; 'bowed and prostrated herself' with qāḏaḏ, 1 Kgs 1:16, 31); they did not cite this entry. Josh 5:14 (wayyištāḥû before the captain of the host of YHWH) is aligned with the Kings rendering, 'bowed down' (ASV 'did worship'). The plain verb is preferred here because the choice is interpretively loaded: 'worshipped' would decide that Yehoshua takes the figure for YHWH, and 'made obeisance' would suggest homage to a human superior, while the narrative leaves the figure's status open (Nelson, Joshua, OTL, 1997). The clause records the Kings practice so that the entry governs the verb across the Former Prophets; the Kings sites printing 'bowed down' are added to appliesTo, and the Kings paragraphs may receive this id in glossaryRefs at their next pass.
